          "grounding_supports": [
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 108,
                "end_index": 280,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "While a whisper sounds like a softer version of your normal voice, biologically, it is a completely different mechanism of speech production known as **unvoiced phonation**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2,
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 317,
                "end_index": 539,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The Whispered Voice\nTo understand what happens when you whisper, it helps to first look at how you speak normally:\n* **Normal Speech (Voiced Phonation):** Your brain signals your vocal folds (vocal cords) to close (adduct)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2,
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 541,
                "end_index": 588,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "As you exhale, air pressure builds beneath them"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 590,
                "end_index": 761,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When the air forces its way through, it causes the vocal folds to rapidly open and close in a cyclic, vibrating motion (driven by elastic tension and the Bernoulli effect)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 763,
                "end_index": 854,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This rapid vibration creates periodic sound waves, which give your voice its pitch and tone"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 856,
                "end_index": 933,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Whispering (Unvoiced Phonation):** Your vocal folds do not vibrate at all"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 935,
                "end_index": 1023,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Instead, they are held in a specific, rigid position that prevents them from oscillating"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6,
                1,
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1034,
                "end_index": 1385,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The Shape of the \"Whispering Glottis\"\nWhen you decide to whisper, the muscles in your larynx (voice box) change the physical configuration of your vocal folds:\n* **The Inverted \"Y\" Shape:** The lateral cricoarytenoid muscles pull the front (anterior) and middle thirds of your true vocal folds tightly together, compressing them so they cannot vibrate"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1387,
                "end_index": 1531,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The Posterior Glottal Gap:** At the very back of your larynx, the arytenoid cartilages (which anchor the vocal folds) remain slightly parted"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1533,
                "end_index": 1628,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This leaves a small, triangular opening called the **posterior glottal gap** (or glottal chink)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1631,
                "end_index": 1730,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* Taken together, the closed front and open back of your vocal folds form an **inverted \"Y\" shape**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1740,
                "end_index": 1927,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The Physics of the Sound: Creating Turbulence\nBecause the front of your vocal folds is closed, all the air you exhale from your lungs is forced through the tiny triangular gap at the back"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1930,
                "end_index": 2059,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* Instead of the smooth, cyclic vibrations of normal speech, the air rushing through this narrow gap becomes highly **turbulent**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2061,
                "end_index": 2180,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* This turbulence creates a chaotic, aperiodic \"white noise\" (friction sound) similar to wind blowing through a keyhole"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2317,
                "end_index": 2552,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* While the *generator* of the sound changes (from vibrating vocal folds to turbulent air), your **supralaryngeal articulators**\u2014your throat, tongue, palate, teeth, and lips\u2014move exactly the same way they do when you speak normally"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                10,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2555,
                "end_index": 2681,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* These structures shape the turbulent, rushing air, filtering specific frequencies to form recognizable vowels and consonants"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2684,
                "end_index": 2785,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* *(Note: Because your vocal cords aren't vibrating, whispered speech has no true vocal pitch or tone"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1,
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2787,
                "end_index": 3014,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Distinguishing between \"voiced\" and \"unvoiced\" consonants\u2014like the difference between a \"V\" and an \"F\", or a \"Z\" and an \"S\"\u2014becomes much harder, and your mouth has to make subtle, hyper-articulated adjustments to compensate"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                12,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3024,
                "end_index": 3199,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "### Why Whispering Strains Your Voice\nThere is a common biological misconception that whispering is a good way to \"rest\" your voice when you have laryngitis or a hoarse throat"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3238,
                "end_index": 3362,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Holding the front of your vocal folds tightly closed while keeping the back open requires intense, unnatural muscular effort"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6,
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3364,
                "end_index": 3560,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "To achieve this, we often engage in **laryngeal hyperfunction**\u2014constricting the throat and squeezing the surrounding \"false vocal folds\" (muscular folds located just above the true vocal folds)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                12
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3564,
                "end_index": 3760,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This excessive muscular squeezing and the dry, high-velocity air rushing through the small glottal gap can cause **more vocal fatigue and irritation** than speaking in a soft, gentle, normal voice"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                12
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3762,
                "end_index": 3916,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "If you ever need to rest your voice, ENTs and voice therapists recommend either total silence or speaking softly at a normal pitch, rather than whispering"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            }
          ],
